atient who is  suffering from systemic lupus edit  matters and based on this particular  characteristic rashes  in her face you can understand it might  be a symptom of loop lupus  so in order to get 100 percent sure the  doctor prescribes an anarchist as a  beginning  so in that particular test blood samples  would be drawn from the patient the  serum would be extracted and the serum  would be added to a hep 2 cell line in a  pathological laboratory  now hip to cell line is an array of  hundreds of antigens  now if the antinuclear antibody is  present most likely these antibodies  would detect antigens present on these  hepta cell lines hepta cells are used  because they have a wide variety of  these antigens and and these varieties  are not recapitulated in many other cell  lines but luckily hepta cell line does  so we have two eye outcomes one outcome  is the patient sample has  antinuclear antibodies and second  outcome is the patient sample might not  have any anti-nuclear antibody  so obviously  the second case  the ana test would be negative and in  the first case the ana test would be  positive  so  what we do is after incubating the hep  to cell line with patient derived serum  we add secondary antibodies so let's say  particular  anti-nuclear antibody was present  and against that a secondary antibody is  used which is fluorophoretic so if that  primary antibody was present in the  patient sample only then the secondary  antibody would bind to the primary  antibody and it would show the  fluorescence  otherwise it would be washed away in the  washing stem  ultimately scientists actually  look at these particular data under  fluorescence microscope so by looking at  the cells under fluorescence microscope  pathologists can understand what is the  scenario there are many  staining patterns that can be obtained  such as homogeneous where the overall  fluorescence is coming from all the  parts of the cell there could be  speckled pattern where the fluorescence